## Summary

I fixed a formatter idempotence bug in Biome's JavaScript formatter affecting switch statements with trailing line comments on fall-through case clauses.

### Root cause

In `crates/biome_js_formatter/src/js/auxiliary/case_clause.rs`, when a case clause's consequent is a single block statement, the formatter lets the block "hug" the case line:

```js
case b: { ... }
```

The trailing line comment on the case is rendered as a *line suffix*, which floats to the end of the line — landing **after** the opening brace:

```js
case b: { // comment b
  doStuff();
}
```

On a second format pass, that comment now parses as being *inside* the block, so it gets moved onto its own line — a non-idempotent result and a violation of "comments must not be moved into a subsequent block statement":

```js
case b: {
  // comment b
  doStuff();
}
```

### The fix

I disable block-hugging when the case clause has a trailing line comment (detected via `f.comments().has_trailing_line_comment(test.syntax())`). The block then breaks onto its own indented line, keeping the comment on the case line:

```js
switch (key) {
  case a: // comment a
  case b: // comment b
    {
      doStuff();
    }
}
```

This output is stable across repeated format passes because reparsing keeps the comment as trailing trivia on the `case b:` line (before the newline), so the same "has trailing line comment → don't hug" decision fires again.

### Verification

- Reproduced the original non-idempotence with a `CheckReformat` failure via `quick_test`, then confirmed the fix makes it idempotent in **both 2-space and tab** indentation modes.
- Full `biome_js_formatter` suite: **273 passed, 0 failed** — no regressions.
- Only source (`case_clause.rs`) was modified; nothing in `tests/` was touched (`quick_test.rs` was restored to its original content).
